 \section{Limitations and Future Work}
-\textbf{Evaluation split.} All segmentation numbers come from the LoveDA validation split, which also guided epoch selection, so no held-out test set was used and results carry model-selection bias. No local baselines or ablation. Figures for other methods (Sect.~2) come from their papers rather than identical retraining here, and isolating the FPN, ASPP, and decoder's individual contributions is left for future work; this is a demonstration, not proof of outperforming any baseline.
-\textbf{Planning scope and failure modes.} Results cover only the ten selected LoveDA tiles (Table~\ref{tab:tile10}); 100\% connectivity is not a general guarantee and fails outright where no road pixels are detected (tiles 4219, 4222), and no proposed route has been checked for engineering, legal, terrain, environmental, cost, or social feasibility -- the Bresenham fallback can cross impassable terrain. \textbf{Heuristic costs and units.} A$^{*}$ costs are heuristic, not calibrated construction costs, and distances remain in pixels rather than the georeferenced units OSMnx~\cite{boeing2017} could provide. \textbf{Generalisation.} Ten tiles is a small sample for judging generalisation; segmentation errors propagate downstream, and Rural/Urban LoveDA domains were trained jointly without separate evaluation.
